---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How Black Ops Archive selects, verifies, and presents documented intelligence operations. Scope, status definitions, and sourcing standards."
>
  <NavBar currentPath="/about" />

  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-10">

    <div class="mb-10 pb-8 border-b border-[#1e1e1e]">
      <span class="stamp-classified mb-4 inline-block">About This Archive</span>
      <h1 class="font-stencil text-4xl text-classified-green tracking-wide mb-3 mt-3">Methodology</h1>
      <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-dim tracking-widest uppercase">
        Scope, sourcing standards, and how records are presented
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">

      <h2>Scope</h2>
      <p>
        Black Ops Archive covers documented intelligence operations conducted by or involving the following services and their historical predecessors:
      </p>
      <p class="font-mono text-[13px] text-mid">
        CIA, MI6, Mossad, NSA, FBI, GCHQ, KGB/FSB, BND, Joint Chiefs of Staff, and allied or predecessor organizations (OSS, SOE, etc.).
      </p>
      <p>
        Content is limited to <strong>documented history</strong>. No paranormal, extraterrestrial, or unverified fringe claims. Every operation included has a primary source trail: Senate or parliamentary reports, FOIA-released documents, court records, official government acknowledgements, or mainstream investigative journalism that draws directly on such records.
      </p>

      <h2>Status Definitions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-classified-green">Confirmed</strong> — The operation (or its core elements) has been officially acknowledged by a government, agency, or parliamentary body, or is supported by an unbroken chain of declassified primary documents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-classified-red">Alleged</strong> — Credible reporting, participant testimony, or substantial circumstantial evidence exists, but there is no official governmental or agency acknowledgement and key documents remain classified or missing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-amber-500">Disputed</strong> — Official records conflict, multiple governments or agencies have offered contradictory accounts, or significant credible evidence challenges the dominant narrative.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sourcing Standards</h2>
      <p>
        Every factual claim in an article must be attributable to at least one primary or near-primary source. Preference order:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Official declassified documents (FOIA releases, NSAEBB collections, congressional reports, court filings)</li>
        <li>Senate, parliamentary, or independent commission reports (Church Committee, Pike Committee, European Parliament resolutions, etc.)</li>
        <li>Official acknowledgements by heads of state, ministers, or agency directors</li>
        <li>High-quality investigative journalism that cites and links primary documents</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Speculation beyond what the sources support is not included. When an aspect of an operation remains genuinely contested, the article states the range of evidence and the official position.
      </p>

      <h2>Related Operations</h2>
      <p>
        Each article page shows a "Related Operations" section. When an article declares explicit <code>related</code> slugs in its frontmatter, those are shown first. Otherwise the site automatically surfaces operations that share the same agency or era, scored to prioritize the strongest overlaps. This creates a lightweight connection graph without requiring a full database.
      </p>

      <h2>Archive, Map, and Filters</h2>
      <p>
        The interactive map and the Archive listing are both generated from the same content collection. Status, era, and agency filters on the Archive page are client-side and fully URL-addressable so filtered views can be bookmarked or shared. The map offers both conventional pin clustering and a "Network" mode that draws connections from agency headquarters to operations.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections and Additions</h2>
      <p>
        Primary sources are sometimes re-interpreted as more documents are released. If you have additional verified primary sources or corrections to existing articles, the preferred channel is a public issue or pull request on the project repository, or direct contact with the maintainer. All accepted changes are attributed in the article history where appropriate.
      </p>

      <p class="text-dim text-[12px] mt-8">
        This site is a personal project by Steven Baird. It is not affiliated with any government, intelligence service, or political organization.
      </p>

    </div>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>